  The kernel is instead **extracted** so that one implementation exists in
  the profile and the VM module consumes it:
  - **Owner**: `dao.host.float` (over `dao.host.integer` only; no `yin`
    require). The kernel is written entirely over the shim today (V: every
    helper calls `host/*`, `integer.cljc:156-163, 394-440, 487-493,
    580-586, 648-710`), so the move is a cut and paste under a new
    namespace.
  - **What moves** (P, all `defn-` today): `ratio->float` (`:434-464`:
    nearest-even binary64 of `x / y`, the one rounding step), `pow2`,
    `signed`, `trunc-big`, `float-ratio`, `decimal-exponent`, `scaled`,
    `pow10` and `small-powers-of-ten`, `digits->big`, `strip-trailing-zeros`,
    `sign`, `bits`, and the two kernels `decimal->float` (`:769-802`
    without its refusal preamble) and `float-digits` (`:721-759`). They
    become public functions of `dao.host.float` under these exact
    signatures: `(decimal->float digit-values exp10)` takes a vector of
    digit values 0-9 and an exact-integer exponent (any carrier) and
    answers a host double: the nearest-even binary64 of
    `digits x 10^exp10`, `+inf` past the range, `0.0` below it, never a
    refusal (syntax is the caller's); `(float-digits v)` takes a finite
    nonzero host double and answers `[digits-string exp10]`, the shortest
    round-tripping decimal (CPython `repr` digits); `(ratio->float x y
    negative?)` as today. **Table B composition** `decimal-text->float`
    (text `digits[.digits]` to digit values and `-(fraction length)`, then
    `decimal->float`; refuses any other text) sits beside them.
  - **How `yin.vm.integer` consumes it (H2)**: `int-decimal->float` and
    `int-float-digits` keep their arity, type and syntax refusals
    (`wrong-type!`, `refuse!`, `digit-values!`, `float!`) and delegate the
    arithmetic to `dao.host.float/decimal->float` and `float-digits`;
    `int-to-float`, `int-true-div` and `int-from-float` call the moved
    `ratio->float` and `trunc-big`. One implementation, in the profile;
    the VM module is its first consumer and `yang.php` its second.
  - **Where it is counted**: nowhere in the 76. The moved functions carry
    no reader arm; they are compositions over `dao.host.integer` entries
    and are listed under Table B as the "float-text kernel" (one named
    item), which keeps Table A's count honest (R7-1).
  - **Oracle that proves equality with today**: `integer_test.cljc`
    `float-fixture-test`, `float-named-cases-test`,
    `float-text-named-cases-test`, `float-refusals-test` and
    `int_contract_test.cljc` `float-rows-recompute-on-this-host`,
    `text-round-trips-through-the-module` (V, CPython-generated rows): H2
    runs them unchanged through the rewired module, so any arithmetic
    difference fails; H1 additionally runs the fixture rows **directly**
    against `dao.host.float` in `dao.host.float-test`, plus the
    `decimal-text->float` cases `"1.5"`, `"0.1"`, `"9007199254740993.0"`
    (rounds to `9007199254740992.0`), a 400-digit integer text (`+inf`)
    and `"1."`.
  - **Slices**: H1 creates `dao.host.float` with the kernel copied in and
    tested directly (the VM module still holds its own copy, so no
    behaviour changes); H2 rewires `yin.vm.integer` to delegate and
    deletes its copy, and switches `yang/php.cljc:95-97` to
    `decimal-text->float`. `Double/parseDouble`, `js/parseFloat` and Dart
    `double.parse` are nearest-even on this grammar (R), so the PHP float
    path is not a behaviour change; the integer path at `:98-100` is (§10).
- `dao.host.text/code-points` is callable from `dao.host` with no `yin`
  require (V): the private `data.cljc:143-159` is written over
  `unit-count` and `code-unit` only, which are Table A text entries; its
  surrogate cases exist in `data_test.cljc` (`non-bmp-code-points-test`).

**Table C: runtime prerequisites (documented and verified by existing
suites; not profile entries; not a boundary).**

+----------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------+
| Prerequisite                     | Where, and the law a port must meet (V)                                                        | Slice   |
+==================================+================================================================================================+=========+
| `deftype` with per-host          | `values.cljc:39-119`: `Closure`/`Continuation` equal iff owner and payload equal; hash of      | H1 docs |
| equality, hashing and printing   | `[:tag owner payload]`; print the marker text. `cbor.cljc:342-460`: `Float64` equal by bits    |         |
|                                  | with NaN equal to NaN (`:365-367`); `Rational`, `Decimal` equal by fields; hashes at           |         |
|                                  | `:372-444`.                                                                                    |         |
+----------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------+
| Atomic flag                      | `btree.cljc:87-119`: transient ownership token readable across threads.                        | none    |
+----------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------+
| Weak and soft references         | `btree.cljc:182-245`; default `:soft` on the JVM, `:strong` on JS and Dart (`:39-44`). A host  | none    |
|                                  | without them declares `:strong`; correctness is unchanged, retention differs.                  |         |
+----------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------+
| `locking`, atoms, volatiles      | Five `locking` sites (§3.1); single-owner discipline elsewhere.                                | none    |
+----------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------+
| Host promise type                | `btree/storage.cljc:218-227`: deferred (§9.1).                                                 | defer   |
+----------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------+
| Dialect syntax                   | §3.3.                                                                                          | H0      |
+----------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------+

Layering: `dao.host.*` requires nothing in the repo; `dao` never requires
`yin`. Consumers after H2: `dao.jing`, `dao.jing.cbor`, `yin.vm.integer`,
`yin.vm.data`, `yin.vm.values`, `yin.vm.engine`, `yin.vm`, `yin.vm.debruijn`,
`yin.vm.debruijn-code`, `dao.stream.chunks`, `dao.jing.dht`, `yin.vm.linker`,
`yin.vm.linker.sign`, `dao.stream.datagram`, `dao.stream.base64`, and the
three Python integer tests.

(ns dao.host.bytes
  "The host byte arrays of the primitive profile
   (docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md §4.2, table A): `byte[]` on
   the JVM, `Uint8Array` on JS, `Uint8List` on Dart. A byte is read and
   written as a native integer 0 to 255 on every host.

   P entries (§2.4): `bytes?`, `alloc`, `length`, `get`, `slice`,
   `equal?`, `from-vector`, `to-vector`. They leave every argument
   byte-identical; `alloc`, `slice` and `from-vector` return fresh
   storage, never a view, and the caller holds the exclusive mutation
   right to it until it is published (§2.4).

   M entries: `set!` and `copy!` mutate exactly their named target, never
   retain it, and return nil. Every index, length and byte value is
   checked before any write, so a refused call leaves the target
   byte-identical. `copy!` takes `(src src-pos dest dest-pos len)`, the
   `System/arraycopy` shape of `dao.data.arrays/acopy`, and behaves as
   memmove when `src` and `dest` are one array.

   Oracle: `dao.host.bytes-test` (table D). A refusal throws `ex-info`
   whose data carries `:dao.host/refusal` (`:out-of-range` for an index,
   length or byte value, `:unsupported-value` for a non-byte-array) and
   `:dao.host/entry`. Every reader conditional names `:cljd` first,
   because the ClojureDart build also reads `:clj`."
  (:refer-clojure :exclude [bytes? get set!])
  #?(:cljd (:import ["dart:typed_data" Uint8List])))


(defn- refuse
  [entry cls detail]
  (throw (ex-info (str "dao.host refused: " entry " " (name cls) " (" detail ")")
                  {:dao.host/refusal cls :dao.host/entry entry :detail detail})))


(defn bytes?
  "P. True when `x` is a host byte array."
  [x]
  #?(:cljd (dart/is? x Uint8List)
     :clj (clojure.core/bytes? x)
     :cljs (instance? js/Uint8Array x)))


(defn- native-int?
  [x]
  #?(:cljd (dart/is? x int)
     :clj (or (instance? Long x) (instance? Integer x)
              (instance? Short x) (instance? Byte x))
     :cljs (js/Number.isSafeInteger x)))


(defn- check-bytes!
  [entry bs]
  (when-not (bytes? bs)
    (refuse entry :unsupported-value "not a host byte array")))


(defn- check-index!
  "Refuse unless native integer `i` lies in [lo, hi]."
  [entry i lo hi]
  (when-not (and (native-int? i) (<= lo i) (<= i hi))
    (refuse entry :out-of-range (str i " not in [" lo ", " hi "]"))))


(defn- check-byte!
  [entry v]
  (when-not (and (native-int? v) (<= 0 v) (<= v 255))
    (refuse entry :out-of-range (str v " is not a byte 0-255"))))


(defn length
  "P. The number of bytes in `bs`."
  [bs]
  (check-bytes! 'length bs)
  #?(:cljd (.-length ^Uint8List bs)
     :clj (alength ^bytes bs)
     :cljs (.-length bs)))


(defn alloc
  "P, fresh. A new host byte array of `n` zero bytes, `n` a native
   integer from 0."
  [n]
  (when-not (and (native-int? n) (<= 0 n))
    (refuse 'alloc :out-of-range (str n " is not a length")))
  #?(:cljd (Uint8List. n)
     :clj (byte-array (int n))
     :cljs (js/Uint8Array. n)))


(defn- byte-at
  "The byte at an index already checked."
  [bs i]
  #?(:cljd (. ^Uint8List bs "[]" i)
     :clj (bit-and (aget ^bytes bs (int i)) 0xff)
     :cljs (aget bs i)))


(defn get
  "P. The byte at index `i` of `bs`, 0 to 255."
  [bs i]
  (check-index! 'get i 0 (dec (length bs)))
  (byte-at bs i))


(defn slice
  "P, fresh. A new host byte array holding `bs[start, end)`, for
   0 <= start <= end <= length."
  [bs start end]
  (let [n (length bs)]
    (check-index! 'slice start 0 n)
    (check-index! 'slice end start n))
  #?(:cljd (Uint8List.fromList (.sublist ^Uint8List bs start end))
     :clj (java.util.Arrays/copyOfRange ^bytes bs (int start) (int end))
     :cljs (.slice bs start end)))


(defn equal?
  "P. True when byte arrays `a` and `b` hold the same bytes."
  [a b]
  (let [n (length a)]
    (and (= n (length b))
         (loop [i 0]
           (or (= i n)
               (and (= (byte-at a i) (byte-at b i))
                    (recur (inc i))))))))


(defn from-vector
  "P, fresh. A new host byte array of `xs`, a vector of native integers
   0 to 255; any other element is refused `:out-of-range` before
   anything is built."
  [xs]
  (when-not (vector? xs)
    (refuse 'from-vector :unsupported-value "not a vector"))
  (run! #(check-byte! 'from-vector %) xs)
  #?(:cljd (Uint8List.fromList xs)
     :clj (byte-array (map unchecked-byte xs))
     :cljs (js/Uint8Array.from (into-array xs))))


(defn to-vector
  "P. The bytes of `bs` as a vector of native integers 0 to 255."
  [bs]
  (let [n (length bs)]
    (loop [i 0
           out (transient [])]
      (if (< i n)
        (recur (inc i) (conj! out (byte-at bs i)))
        (persistent! out)))))


(defn set!
  "M. Write byte `v`, 0 to 255, at index `i` of target `bs`; nil. A bad
   index or byte is refused before the write."
  [bs i v]
  (check-index! 'set! i 0 (dec (length bs)))
  (check-byte! 'set! v)
  #?(:cljd (. ^Uint8List bs "[]=" i v)
     :clj (aset ^bytes bs (int i) (unchecked-byte v))
     :cljs (aset bs i v))
  nil)


(defn copy!
  "M. Copy `len` bytes from `src[src-pos ...]` into target
   `dest[dest-pos ...]`; nil. Both ranges are checked before any write,
   and overlapping ranges in one array copy as memmove."
  [src src-pos dest dest-pos len]
  (let [sn (length src)
        dn (length dest)]
    (check-index! 'copy! len 0 (min sn dn))
    (check-index! 'copy! src-pos 0 (- sn len))
    (check-index! 'copy! dest-pos 0 (- dn len)))
  #?(:cljd (.setRange ^Uint8List dest dest-pos (+ dest-pos len)
                      (if (identical? src dest)
                        (.sublist ^Uint8List src src-pos (+ src-pos len))
                        src)
                      (if (identical? src dest) 0 src-pos))
     :clj (System/arraycopy ^bytes src (int src-pos) ^bytes dest (int dest-pos) (int len))
     :cljs (.set dest (.subarray src src-pos (+ src-pos len)) dest-pos))
  nil)
(ns dao.host.text
  "The host strings of the primitive profile
   (docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md §4.2, table A). A host string
   is a sequence of UTF-16 code units on every host (JVM `String`, JS
   string, Dart `String`).

   Table A, kind P (§2.4): `unit-count`, `code-unit`, `units->string`,
   `utf8-encode`, `utf8-decode`, `nfc`. Equal arguments give equal
   results on every host:
   - `utf8-encode` refuses an unpaired surrogate `:unpaired-surrogate`
     instead of substituting (the JVM's `?`, U+FFFD elsewhere), so the
     bytes of every string it accepts are identical on every host; it
     returns fresh bytes the caller holds the mutation right to.
   - `utf8-decode` is strict RFC 3629: an overlong form, an encoded
     surrogate, a code point above U+10FFFF or a truncated sequence is
     refused `:invalid-utf8`, never decoded to U+FFFD; a leading BOM is
     content. It is the decoder of `dao.jing.cbor` (`utf8`), extracted.
   - `nfc` is Unicode Normalization Form C (`java.text.Normalizer`,
     `String.prototype.normalize`, the `unorm_dart` package); the profile
     pins Unicode 16.0 (§2.5).

   Table B, P, no host arm: `code-points`, over `unit-count` and
   `code-unit` (the private `yin.vm.data` helper, made public here).

   Oracle: `dao.host.text-test` and the text rows of table D, re-asserted
   by `dao.host.conformance-test`. A refusal throws `ex-info` whose data
   carries `:dao.host/refusal`, a class keyword, and `:dao.host/entry`.
   Every reader conditional names `:cljd` first, because the ClojureDart
   build also reads `:clj`."
  #?@(:cljd [(:require ["dart:convert" :as convert]
                       ["package:unorm_dart/unorm_dart.dart" :as unorm])
             (:import ["dart:core" FormatException String]
                      ["dart:typed_data" Uint8List])]
      :clj [(:import (java.nio ByteBuffer)
                     (java.nio.charset CharacterCodingException CodingErrorAction
                                       StandardCharsets))]))


(defn- refuse
  [entry cls detail]
  (throw (ex-info (str "dao.host refused: " entry " " (name cls) " (" detail ")")
                  {:dao.host/refusal cls :dao.host/entry entry :detail detail})))


;; =============================================================================
;; Code units
;; =============================================================================

(defn unit-count
  "P. The number of UTF-16 code units of string `s`."
  [s]
  #?(:cljd (.-length ^String s)
     :clj (.length ^String s)
     :cljs (.-length s)))


(defn code-unit
  "P. The UTF-16 code unit at index `i` of `s`, 0 to 0xFFFF; the caller
   has checked 0 <= i < `unit-count`."
  [s i]
  #?(:cljd (.codeUnitAt ^String s i)
     :clj (int (.charAt ^String s (int i)))
     :cljs (.charCodeAt s i)))


(defn units->string
  "P. The host string of the UTF-16 code units `units` (each 0 to
   0xFFFF), unpaired surrogates included."
  [units]
  #?(:cljd (let [sb (StringBuffer)]
             (doseq [u units] (.writeCharCode sb u))
             (.toString sb))
     :clj (let [sb (StringBuilder.)]
            (doseq [u units] (.append sb (char u)))
            (.toString sb))
     :cljs (let [a (array)]
             (doseq [u units] (.push a (.fromCharCode js/String u)))
             (.join a ""))))


(defn- high-surrogate?
  [u]
  (<= 0xD800 u 0xDBFF))


(defn- low-surrogate?
  [u]
  (<= 0xDC00 u 0xDFFF))


(defn code-points
  "P, table B. The code points of string `s` as a vector: a surrogate
   pair decodes to one code point, an unpaired surrogate is its own."
  [s]
  (let [n (unit-count s)]
    (loop [i 0
           out (transient [])]
      (if (< i n)
        (let [u (code-unit s i)
              v (when (< (inc i) n) (code-unit s (inc i)))]
          (if (and (high-surrogate? u) v (low-surrogate? v))
            (recur (+ i 2)
                   (conj! out (+ 0x10000
                                 (* (- u 0xD800) 0x400)
                                 (- v 0xDC00))))
            (recur (inc i) (conj! out u))))
        (persistent! out)))))


;; =============================================================================
;; UTF-8
;; =============================================================================

(defn- check-surrogates!
  "Refuse `:unpaired-surrogate` unless every surrogate of `s` is paired."
  [entry s]
  (let [n (unit-count s)]
    (loop [i 0]
      (when (< i n)
        (let [c (code-unit s i)]
          (cond
            (high-surrogate? c)
            (if (and (< (inc i) n) (low-surrogate? (code-unit s (inc i))))
              (recur (+ i 2))
              (refuse entry :unpaired-surrogate (str "unpaired high surrogate at " i)))
            (low-surrogate? c)
            (refuse entry :unpaired-surrogate (str "unpaired low surrogate at " i))
            :else (recur (inc i))))))))


(defn utf8-encode
  "P, fresh. The UTF-8 bytes of string `s`. A string holding an unpaired
   surrogate is refused `:unpaired-surrogate` before any host encoder
   sees it."
  [s]
  (when-not (string? s)
    (refuse 'utf8-encode :unsupported-value "not a string"))
  (check-surrogates! 'utf8-encode s)
  #?(:cljd (convert/utf8.encode s)
     :clj (.getBytes ^String s StandardCharsets/UTF_8)
     :cljs (.encode (js/TextEncoder.) s)))


(defn- byte-count
  [bs]
  #?(:cljd (.-length ^Uint8List bs)
     :clj (alength ^bytes bs)
     :cljs (.-length bs)))


(defn utf8-decode
  "P. The string of the UTF-8 bytes `bs`, or of `bs[start, start+len)`.
   Strict: any ill-formed sequence is refused `:invalid-utf8`, never
   substituted; a range outside `bs` is refused `:out-of-range`."
  ([bs] (utf8-decode bs 0 (byte-count bs)))
  ([bs start len]
   (let [n (byte-count bs)]
     (when-not (and (int? start) (int? len) (<= 0 start) (<= 0 len) (<= (+ start len) n))
       (refuse 'utf8-decode :out-of-range (str "[" start ", +" len ") outside " n " bytes"))))
   ;; Dart's decoder drops a U+FEFF at the start of what it decodes; a BOM
   ;; is content here, so leading BOM bytes are taken one by one and the
   ;; decoder only ever starts after them.
   #?(:cljd (let [end (+ start len)]
              (loop [at start boms 0]
                (if (and (<= (+ at 3) end)
                         (= 0xef (. ^Uint8List bs "[]" at))
                         (= 0xbb (. ^Uint8List bs "[]" (+ at 1)))
                         (= 0xbf (. ^Uint8List bs "[]" (+ at 2))))
                  (recur (+ at 3) (inc boms))
                  (str (apply str (repeat boms (String.fromCharCode 0xfeff)))
                       (try
                         (.convert (convert/Utf8Decoder.) bs at end)
                         (catch FormatException e
                           (refuse 'utf8-decode :invalid-utf8 (str e))))))))
      :clj (try
             (str (.decode (-> StandardCharsets/UTF_8
                               .newDecoder
                               (.onMalformedInput CodingErrorAction/REPORT)
                               (.onUnmappableCharacter CodingErrorAction/REPORT))
                           (ByteBuffer/wrap ^bytes bs (int start) (int len))))
             (catch CharacterCodingException e
               (refuse 'utf8-decode :invalid-utf8 (.getMessage e))))
      :cljs (try
              (.decode (js/TextDecoder. "utf-8"
                                        #js {:fatal true :ignoreBOM true})
                       (.subarray bs start (+ start len)))
              (catch :default e
                (refuse 'utf8-decode :invalid-utf8 (str e)))))))


;; =============================================================================
;; Normalization
;; =============================================================================

(defn nfc
  "P. Unicode Normalization Form C of string `s`."
  [s]
  #?(:cljd (unorm/nfc s)
     :clj (java.text.Normalizer/normalize ^String s java.text.Normalizer$Form/NFC)
     :cljs (.normalize s "NFC")))

